{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>To Leah his daughter, a maidservant.</b> It is difficult why it was necessary to say ״to Leah his daughter״ and it would not have sufficed with what it said nearby: ״and Laban gave her״ — for it is self-evident that he is speaking about Leah. What can one say — that it is not self-evident? If so, why did he write the word ״her״ beforehand? Further, why did it say ״a maidservant״ and not say ״for a maidservant״?" "<b>Now</b> it may be explained according to the way of those masters of blessed memory: Zilpah and Bilhah fell to Rachel and Leah as inheritance from their mother's dowry. And this is what the verse says — <b>to Leah his daughter, a maidservant</b> — meaning, the verse informs us that the maidservant was hers, for she inherited her from her mother. Therefore the verse was precise in saying <b>his daughter</b>, to hint that since she was his daughter, she acquired this maidservant from her mother's dowry — not that she acquired it through some other means. And along this line one may also examine precisely the verse's saying <b>and Laban gave</b> — for there was no need to mention his name, since it is understood from the phrase ״to Leah his daughter״ that the verse speaks of Laban; rather, it comes to say that this gift was not truly a gift, for the maidservant had been hers already beforehand, but it was Laban who presented it as though in the category of a gift — and this is hinted in the word <b>his daughter</b>."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:53.071843478Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}